            <ArticleTitle>A case of variant angina in a patient under chronic treatment with sorafenib.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>476-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nrclinonc.2010.67</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 63-year-old man with an unresectable multifocal hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) presented with upper abdominal discomfort, nausea and vomiting. We report a case of variant angina in a patient affected by unresectable HCC under chronic treatment with sorafenib. Spontaneous spasm occurred during cardiac catheterization and was revealed during coronary angiogram with the unusual feature of a retrograde transient filling of a contralateral branch.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Electrocardiogram, cardiac catheterization, chest X-ray, emergency ECG.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Variant angina induced by sorafenib treatment mimicking infero-posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">High-dose calcium-antagonists and nitrates were initially given intravenously and then orally. Sorafenib therapy was then resumed without further symptoms. Restaging of the cancer revealed unexpected local recurrence and the patient died 1 month after receiving palliative care. We contend that the effects of sorafenib treatment were primarily responsible for the major cardiovascular event observed in this case, and it is important for clinicians to be aware of this possible severe complication of sorafenib therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
